---
import {isOpen} from '../../lib/pattern-experiment';
interface Props {kind:'input'|'pursuit'}
const {kind}=Astro.props;
---
{kind==='input'?<section class="technique-experiment" id="experiment" data-input-experiment aria-labelledby="experiment-title">
 <div class="experiment-intro"><h2 id="experiment-title">Hold it. Sample it. Try it again.</h2><p>Choose the held controls, then take a sample. Hold Fire across several samples: there should be one new press, and one sound event.</p><p>This is an original browser example. It does not read a historical machine’s ports or emulate its sound chip.</p></div>
 <fieldset><legend>Controls held now</legend><label><input type="checkbox" data-button="left"/>Left</label><label><input type="checkbox" data-button="right"/>Right</label><label><input type="checkbox" data-button="fire"/>Fire</label></fieldset>
 <div class="experiment-actions" hidden data-enhanced><button data-sample>Take a sample</button><button data-seed>Enter with these controls held</button><button data-reset>Reset experiment</button></div>
 <div class="sample-flow" aria-label="The three connected jobs">
  <div><h3>Read</h3><output data-current>00000000</output><p>Pressed bits in this sample</p></div>
  <div><h3>Detect</h3><output data-edges>00000000</output><p>New presses since the last sample</p></div>
  <div><h3>Trigger</h3><output data-events>0</output><p>Sound events from new Fire presses</p></div>
 </div>
 <p class="experiment-key">Our format: Left = 4, Right = 8, Fire = 16. A 1 means held. No machine’s raw register layout is implied.</p>
 <p role="status" data-input-status>No samples yet. Select Fire and take a sample.</p>
 <div class="sound-control" data-enhanced hidden><button data-audio aria-pressed="false">Enable sound</button><span data-audio-status>Sound is off. Events remain visible.</span></div>
 <details><summary>Trace the samples and see the rule</summary><pre><code>new_presses = current AND (NOT previous)
if new_presses includes FIRE:
    trigger_sound()
previous = current</code></pre><p>NOT is limited to eight bits here. Seeding history on entry suppresses the edge of an already-held control; it does not erase its held state.</p><div class="trace-scroll"><table><caption>Most recent input samples</caption><thead><tr><th>Sample</th><th>Previous</th><th>Current</th><th>New</th><th>Sound</th></tr></thead><tbody data-trace></tbody></table></div></details>
 <noscript><p>For Fire held across three samples, the input byte is 16, 16, 16. Starting from zero history, the new-press values are 16, 0, 0: one sound event. The source explanations below work without JavaScript.</p></noscript>
</section>:<section class="technique-experiment" id="experiment" data-pursuit-experiment aria-labelledby="experiment-title">
 <div class="experiment-intro"><h2 id="experiment-title">Same movement rule. A different destination.</h2><p>The player stays at (5, 3), facing Up. Choose a target, inspect the legal turns, then advance the blue pursuer one tile.</p></div>
 <div class="pursuit-controls"><label>Aim at<select data-target><option value="current">The player’s current tile</option><option value="ahead">Two tiles ahead of the player</option></select></label><button data-step hidden data-enhanced>Advance one turn</button><button data-restart hidden data-enhanced>Restart board</button></div>
 <div class="pursuit-layout"><svg viewBox="0 0 540 420" width="540" height="420" role="img" aria-labelledby="board-title board-desc" data-board><title id="board-title">An original pursuit board</title><desc id="board-desc">A blue pursuer starts at column three, row three. The amber player is at column five, row three, facing Up. Walls restrict the legal turns.</desc><g data-grid>{Array.from({length:63},(_,i)=>{const x=i%9,y=Math.floor(i/9);return <rect x={x*60+1} y={y*60+1} width="58" height="58" class={isOpen({x,y})?'board-open':'board-wall'}/>;})}</g><rect data-target-mark x="303" y="183" width="54" height="54" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><path data-player d="M330 194l-14 27h28z"/><circle data-pursuer cx="210" cy="210" r="15"/></svg>
 <div><p class="board-key"><span class="pursuer-key">Blue circle: pursuer</span><span class="player-key">Amber triangle: player</span><span>Dashed square: target</span></p><table><caption>Candidate next turns</caption><thead><tr><th>Turn</th><th>Distance²</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody data-turns></tbody></table><p role="status" data-pursuit-status>Current target: (5, 3). From the starting position, Right is closest.</p></div></div>
 <details><summary>What this experiment leaves out</summary><p>The nearest legal neighbour wins. Ties use Up, Left, Down, Right. Reversal is allowed. There is no whole-route search, chase/scatter clock or historical ghost implementation. The original board and two-tile offset make this a comparison motivated by the study, rather than a reproduction of Pac-Man.</p><p>The target may lie beyond a wall. Choosing a target and finding a route are separate jobs. A local rule can also revisit tiles; it is not a promise of successful navigation.</p></details>
 <noscript><p>At the start, targeting the player chooses Right. Targeting (5, 1), two tiles ahead, makes Up and Right equally close. Up wins the stated tie-break. JavaScript enables stepping and the visual trace.</p></noscript>
</section>}
<script>import '../../scripts/pattern-experiment';</script>
